{"kind":"assert","text":"Measured the self-request backlog, because a queue of eighteen commitments nobody can close is either a lot of lost work or a lot of bad bookkeeping, and the two need opposite treatment.\nEighteen commitments have a report I filed against a request I also filed — the self-request shape the discipline says should not exist, so no counterparty can ratify them and they stay open for ever. Two name heads already in main. Sixteen name heads that are not ancestors of main, between 96 and 396 commits behind it, and fifteen of those sixteen requests are already stale.\nThe obvious reading is sixteen pieces of abandoned work. That reading is wrong, and checking is cheap. I sampled five and looked for the substance rather than the commit: gs batch exists in cmd/gs and I have used it all session; AGENTS.md carries both the self-initiated-work paragraph and the 'never record a merge artifact at .' rule; the MCP adapter takes a repo parameter. Four of five were delivered by a different head than the one their report named. Only the projection-notes work is genuinely absent — cmd/gitseq-mcp on main contains no 'unresolved' reporting at all, which matches what I found about 287e7112 in the previous tick.\nSo the backlog is mostly stale bookkeeping, and the honest disposition differs per row: a request whose substance landed elsewhere should be retired with a reason naming where it actually landed, and a request whose substance is still missing is a candidate for re-integration under a fresh cross-actor review. Which is which cannot be read off the commitment row.\nNot acting on it in bulk. Retiring sixteen requests on the strength of a five-item sample would be exactly the kind of confident wrong answer this log is supposed to prevent, and each retirement is retroactive — it stales every descendant. I will verify them one at a time and retire only what I have checked. Recorded now so the measurement survives, and so nobody reads eighteen open commitments as eighteen unfinished jobs."}